    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>'s invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> on 20 July 1974, after a Greek-backed coup there, brought down the junta; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstantinos-karamanlis">Konstantinos Karamanlis</a> returned, the first multiparty elections since 1964 were held, and a 1975 referendum rejected the monarchy.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> became the tenth member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-communities">European Communities</a> in 1981, adopted the euro in January 2001 and hosted the 2004 Summer Olympic Games in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>; New Democracy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasok">PASOK</a> alternated in power for four decades.</li><li>The debt crisis from 2010 brought bailouts of 110 billion and 130 billion euros, a 25% fall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a> between 2009 and 2015 and the emigration of 350,000 to 450,000 people before the bailouts ended in 2018.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Greek merchant fleet, the largest in the world at 18% of global capacity and first in tankers and dry bulk carriers, was built after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> from surplus ships sold off by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> government.</p></aside>
    </section>

